1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:03,372
Servers live in data centers, not on your desk.

2
00:00:03,392 --> 00:00:08,273
To manage one, you reach it over the network, and the tool for that is SSH.

3
00:00:08,743 --> 00:00:12,307
In this video: access remote systems using SSH.

4
00:00:16,327 --> 00:00:18,910
Here's the task, the way an exam would state it.

5
00:00:19,380 --> 00:00:22,026
Access remote systems using SSH.

6
00:00:22,496 --> 00:00:26,224
We'll log in, run commands on another machine, copy files to it,

7
00:00:26,244 --> 00:00:28,215
and read the errors when it fails.

8
00:00:35,735 --> 00:00:37,561
SSH stands for secure shell.

9
00:00:37,581 --> 00:00:41,684
It gives you a shell, or runs a single command, on another machine,

10
00:00:41,704 --> 00:00:43,715
and everything travels encrypted.

11
00:00:47,185 --> 00:00:49,109
The shape is always the same.

12
00:00:49,129 --> 00:00:52,864
The word SSH, then the user you log in as, an at sign,

13
00:00:52,884 --> 00:00:55,144
and the machine's name or address.

14
00:00:58,614 --> 00:01:01,259
After that you can add a command to run there.

15
00:01:01,279 --> 00:01:03,692
Without one, you get an interactive login.

16
00:01:11,212 --> 00:01:12,570
A note about this lab.

17
00:01:12,590 --> 00:01:17,831
We only have one machine, so we connect to localhost, which means this very machine.

18
00:01:17,851 --> 00:01:22,904
For a real server you just use its name or address instead. Nothing else changes.

19
00:01:25,874 --> 00:01:28,946
Also, the first time you connect to a new machine,

20
00:01:28,966 --> 00:01:33,523
SSH asks you to confirm its fingerprint, and then asks for the password.

21
00:01:33,543 --> 00:01:36,739
This video is recorded by a script with no keyboard,

22
00:01:36,759 --> 00:01:40,388
so the lab sets up a key and trusts the machine in advance.

23
00:01:40,408 --> 00:01:44,222
The kit lists those steps, and keys get their own video later.

24
00:01:48,242 --> 00:01:53,588
Here is the full form. SSH, student at localhost, then the command hostname.

25
00:01:53,608 --> 00:01:57,715
It runs on the remote machine and prints its name: node one.

26
00:02:04,185 --> 00:02:09,989
If you leave out the user, SSH uses the name you are logged in as right now. Same result.

27
00:02:12,959 --> 00:02:17,889
Another command: whoami prints the user you are on the remote side. It says student.

28
00:02:21,909 --> 00:02:25,164
Remote servers listen on a port, like a numbered door.

29
00:02:25,184 --> 00:02:29,228
For SSH the default is twenty-two, so you normally never type it.

30
00:02:29,248 --> 00:02:33,473
Dash p lets you choose a different one. Here we spell out the default.

31
00:02:39,993 --> 00:02:44,550
First, we make a small file with echo and the greater-than sign from earlier.

32
00:02:44,570 --> 00:02:46,096
It holds one line of text.

33
00:02:49,066 --> 00:02:53,086
scp means secure copy. It copies files over SSH.

34
00:02:53,106 --> 00:02:56,583
The source comes first, then the destination.

35
00:02:56,603 --> 00:03:02,332
Here the destination is localhost, colon, slash tmp slash hello dot t x t.

36
00:03:02,352 --> 00:03:04,896
Nothing is printed when it works.

37
00:03:07,866 --> 00:03:12,924
Let's prove it arrived. SSH runs cat on the remote side, and there's our line.

38
00:03:15,894 --> 00:03:21,013
Copying the other way is the same command, with the remote file as the source.

39
00:03:21,033 --> 00:03:23,319
We bring it back as copy dot t x t.

40
00:03:26,289 --> 00:03:27,933
And cat shows the same text.

41
00:03:31,953 --> 00:03:35,857
Now, when it fails. A machine name that doesn't exist gives:

42
00:03:35,877 --> 00:03:37,623
could not resolve hostname.

43
00:03:37,643 --> 00:03:41,874
That means a wrong name or a naming problem, not a login problem.

44
00:03:44,844 --> 00:03:48,083
A user that isn't allowed in gives: permission denied.

45
00:03:48,103 --> 00:03:52,910
The machine answered, but it refused you. Wrong user, wrong password, or no key.

46
00:03:56,930 --> 00:04:00,366
Our five steps: task, demo, check, reboot, and your turn.

47
00:04:00,836 --> 00:04:03,596
Files live on disk, so they survive a reboot.

48
00:04:03,616 --> 00:04:06,128
We restart the lab, and it comes back up.

49
00:04:09,598 --> 00:04:12,894
Uptime says zero minutes, so this really is a fresh boot.

50
00:04:12,914 --> 00:04:15,978
The checker later confirms the files are still there.

51
00:04:21,498 --> 00:04:24,593
Your turn. Make a directory called lab five.

52
00:04:24,613 --> 00:04:27,707
Then use SSH to run hostname on localhost.

53
00:04:30,677 --> 00:04:34,834
Send the result into lab five slash remote dash host dot t x t,

54
00:04:34,854 --> 00:04:36,624
with the greater-than sign.

55
00:04:36,644 --> 00:04:40,535
Then run the checker, and try before you open the solution.

56
00:04:48,055 --> 00:04:54,136
Next up: Archive, compress, unpack, and uncompress files using tar, gzip, and bzip2.

57
00:04:54,606 --> 00:04:57,509
Subscribe to follow the series, and let's keep going.
